1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,075
Welcome to Module Seven, and to Unit Three: building an organism.

2
00:00:05,075 --> 00:00:08,162
A genome, on its own, does nothing.

3
00:00:08,162 --> 00:00:09,350
It needs a body.

4
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:11,525
Sense organs to take in the world.

5
00:00:11,525 --> 00:00:12,975
Limbs to act on it.

6
00:00:12,975 --> 00:00:15,625
A metabolism that keeps the cycle going.

7
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:18,837
An immune system that rejects what is dangerous.

8
00:00:18,837 --> 00:00:21,887
And some way to remember what happened yesterday.

9
00:00:21,887 --> 00:00:25,000
In the first unit, you saw how a model is trained.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,537
Those trained weights are the genome.

11
00:00:27,537 --> 00:00:31,050
Today we look at the body, which builders call the harness.

12
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:34,362
The formula you will see again and again is simple.

13
00:00:34,362 --> 00:00:36,975
An agent is a model plus a harness.

14
00:00:36,975 --> 00:00:38,925
Here is why that matters.

15
00:00:38,925 --> 00:00:41,562
The model itself only does one thing.

16
00:00:41,562 --> 00:00:44,050
Text goes in, text comes out.

17
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:45,762
It cannot open a file.

18
00:00:45,762 --> 00:00:47,525
It cannot run a command.

19
00:00:47,525 --> 00:00:49,412
It cannot remember last week.

20
00:00:49,412 --> 00:00:52,987
Every one of those abilities comes from the harness wrapped around it.

21
00:00:52,987 --> 00:00:57,400
The heart of every harness is a loop, and it really is that simple.

22
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,062
Send the model the task and everything that has happened so far.

23
00:01:01,062 --> 00:01:05,225
The model either asks to use a tool, or gives a final answer.

24
00:01:05,225 --> 00:01:11,262
If it asks for a tool, the harness runs it, adds the result to the history, and asks again.

25
00:01:11,262 --> 00:01:16,762
Round and round, until the model stops asking, or the harness says enough.

26
00:01:16,762 --> 00:01:23,812
A team of researchers took apart the source code of eleven real coding agents, including Claude Code and Codex.

27
00:01:23,812 --> 00:01:27,887
They found that every single one is built from the same seven parts.

28
00:01:27,887 --> 00:01:28,750
The loop.

29
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:30,500
The connection to the model.

30
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:31,687
The tools.

31
00:01:31,687 --> 00:01:33,512
Memory and context.

32
00:01:33,512 --> 00:01:35,712
Safety and permissions.

33
00:01:35,712 --> 00:01:37,950
The ability to start helper agents.

34
00:01:37,950 --> 00:01:41,975
And ways to extend it, with things like skills and plugins.

35
00:01:41,975 --> 00:01:45,212
The smallest one is about a hundred lines of code.

36
00:01:45,212 --> 00:01:47,250
The biggest is enormous.

37
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:52,775
And here is the surprise: on benchmarks, the tiny one does about as well.

38
00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:56,650
What the big ones buy is safety, recovery, and control.

39
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:59,750
This anatomy is a powerful diagnostic tool.

40
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:04,000
If your agent keeps redoing yesterday's work, it is missing memory.

41
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,250
If it guesses your conventions, it is missing context.

42
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:11,575
If it hands you broken work and calls it finished, it is missing verification.

43
00:02:11,575 --> 00:02:14,487
Fix the missing layer before you blame the model.

44
00:02:14,487 --> 00:02:16,550
And where does the metaphor fail?

45
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:18,637
A body grows from its genome.

46
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:22,250
The two evolved together and cannot be pulled apart.

47
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,737
A harness is built separately, and you can swap it under the same model.

48
00:02:26,737 --> 00:02:33,575
One team moved their agent from the top thirty to the top five on a public benchmark by changing only the harness.

49
00:02:33,575 --> 00:02:38,400
Imagine moving a genome into a new body and watching it run faster.

50
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:46,900
In today's lab, you will ask your own coding agent to describe its own body, part by part, and to admit which parts it is guessing about.

51
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:49,800
You will save that as a blueprint in your capstone.

52
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,412
Tomorrow, you build a body of your own.

53
00:02:52,412 --> 00:02:54,925
Open your terminal, and let's dissect.

